[Music]  we're back now with a look at how the  coronavirus has devastated small  businesses and their  employees you've probably heard times  are tough but you may not realize just  how tough they are  for small businesses researchers at  harvard university in fact harvard  business school  estimate that one out of every seven  small businesses  as a result of the virus have closed  permanently  think about that earlier this year we  spoke to  employees who were laid off from a small  business in elmsford new york  and the owner who was trying to hold  things together there any way he could  recently we went back to them to see if  anything has changed  it was pretty gut wrenching to be honest  when we met joe gilderson back in march  he just laid off  most of his 20 workers at corporate av  for a company that handles lighting and  sound at large events  he predicted a struggle to make it  through the shutdown if we don't have  work by the fall then  i don't know if we're around at that  point now with the fall just five weeks  away  gilderson says he's still hanging on all  right it's no longer a race  this is going to be a marathon he says  corporate av survived in part because of  funding from the federal government's  paycheck protection program  we kicked the can down the road so to  speak the federal money  bailed you out kept you afloat it's  absolutely  one of the things to keep us afloat the  other was trying to come up with another  way to do things  the company shifted to hosting online  events which bring in less money  and require fewer people that leaves  former employee mo  johnson in much of the same place we  found him months ago  do whatever you can to save the company  save the ship when we get back on the  ship we can go again  what have you been up to since we last  talked to you sitting on the couch  cooking a lot and doing  odd kind of jobs those jobs include  webcasting a high school graduation  as a freelancer for corporate av not an  employee  what's it been like for you  psychologically to go four months  without  being able to say i have a job this is  my job it's weird  my wife and i actually had a spat today  she said  um you know you should go to the dentist  and i'm like we don't have insurance  and it's that's weird i don't care you  should still go it's only 150  and i'm like before it was not a  discussion  now it's like wow the things we have to  put off  more than 5 million laid off workers  lost their health insurance during the  pandemic  and nearly 4 out of every 10 americans  say they face a major threat to their  personal finances  gabenell is among them yeah i don't see  the alive events industry coming back  anytime soon  as he waits for things to pick up again  today's mixtape is called  dreaming and believing he's now hosting  a heavy metal podcast  every morning from his kitchen i guess  it keeps my sanity you know  five months ago when he lost his job at  corporate av  anell and his wife claudia told us they  didn't quite know  how they would move forward so april 1st  are you going to make a mortgage payment  or not  good question we haven't even talked  about that you're just constantly  worried about  like can i make this payment can i put  my kid  you know can i get a doctor even before  even before  since then claudia lost her mother to  covid19  but even as the family deals with that  loss they've been able to control the  financial pain  so far claudia wasn't home when we  stopped by because  she was able to return to her work as a  property manager  after quarantining at home and gabe told  us the federal government's weekly 600  dollar unemployment benefit helped pay  the bills  that benefit of course has since expired  there's nerves there's stress there's  there's a constant anxiety  and i know my wife's feeling the anxiety  too because we know at the end of this  month  we really don't know what we're gonna do  we're literally back to where the last  time you guys were here  wow we don't know what we're gonna do at  the end of this month  there's been laughter he tells us along  with the tears  and he says he is hopeful that things  will turn around  eventually if the work comes back we  come back  and look if i can't go back there  because there's no work  i can't go anywhere to look for work and  what i do  so what kind of timetable do you have in  your mind  for when you might be able to add  employees  with benefits like you had before i  don't really know  i mean i was hoping in fall that was it  was always the plan  so i think you know we're still trying  to keep that ship afloat  yeah we got a long way to go  so guilderson is now predicting that  maybe next spring things will pick up  and i'll be able to rehire his employees  now  i want to put a spotlight on on  something one of his employees said  uh that was mo right in the middle of  the story he talked about putting off  uh dental work because when he lost his  job he lost his health insurance  that is the kind of bleak choice that  millions of people in this country are  facing right now maybe it's not the  doctor it's the rent maybe it's not the  doctor it's the credit card  maybe it's it's lunch they got to skip  it because they got to make it to dinner  and so i think there's really what i  could describe as toxic  uncertainty right now as they wait for  benefit checks yeah the domino effect of  this is huge  and and the uncertainty is it it affects  everybody i was talking to a band  manager  who said he's now planning for no  concerts at all next year  because he has to expect the worst and  hope for the best  i'm so glad you're telling these stories  tony i was two were struck by mo's words  this pandemic is real this coronavirus  is real we're sick of it but it is not  done with us yet  another reminder thank you tony decopol

